## Founding & History
The Stoney Vineyard, which forms the basis of Domaine A, was first planted in 1973 by George and Priscilla Park. It holds the distinction of being the first commercial vineyard in Tasmania's Coal River Valley. In 1989, Peter and Ruth Althaus acquired the Stoney Vineyard, at which point Domaine A "came into its own". The Althauses, a Swiss-born couple, had conducted an extensive worldwide search for an ideal cool-climate site before settling on this location. Their vision was to produce world-class Bordeaux-style blends.

In 2018, Peter and Ruth Althaus retired and sold Domaine A to David Walsh, who is the owner of Moorilla Estate and the Museum of Old and New Art (Mona). Peter Althaus collaborated with Moorilla winemaker Conor van der Reest to ensure a smooth transition before he and Ruth returned to Switzerland in 2019.

## People
The current owner of Domaine A is David Walsh, who also owns Moorilla Estate and Mona. The principal winemaker is Conor van der Reest, who also serves as the winemaker for Moorilla. Van der Reest worked closely with Peter Althaus prior to Althaus's retirement in 2018 and has continued as winemaker since the 2018 vintage. Peter Mueller is the viticulturalist.

The vineyard was initially planted by George and Priscilla Park. From 1989 until 2018, Domaine A was owned and operated by H. Peter and Ruth Althaus.

## Vineyard & Site
Domaine A is situated in the Coal River Valley, Tasmania. The historic Stoney Vineyard is recognized as the oldest in Tasmania's Coal River Valley. The vineyard spans 11 hectares, though one source indicates 14 hectares. It is a single vineyard site.

The vineyard is planted on a north-facing slope, with a North-North Easterly aspect. It benefits from a cold, maritime climate with ample sunlight, experiencing a temperate maritime climate and extended sunlight hours over a long, cool season. The site is noted for having some of the longest sunshine hours in Australia. The soil is characterized by 200-million-year-old Jurassic dolerite.

Varietals planted include red grapes Cabernet Sauvignon, Cabernet Franc, Merlot, Petit Verdot, and Pinot Noir, as well as the white grape Sauvignon Blanc. The farming approach involves close planting in the European tradition, with a density of 6000 vines per hectare. Vines are hand-plucked, hand-tied, and hand-picked during the cool days of autumn.

## Cellar Door Experience
Domaine A features a newly refurbished cellar door that opened in 2022. It operates from Thursday to Sunday, between 11 AM and 5 PM. While walk-ins are welcome subject to availability, bookings for wine tastings are highly advised.

The cellar door offers Bordeaux-inspired wines, which can be paired with curated European-style cheese and charcuterie grazing. Visitors can choose to enjoy a tasting on its own or alongside a curated pantry menu. Expert staff guide visitors through the wines and their stories.

## Food & Hospitality
The cellar door, in collaboration with Larder & Vin, offers curated European-style cheese and charcuterie grazing. Bookings for cheese, charcuterie, or the "Bordeaux Pantry Experience" are essential. The facility also offers alfresco/outdoor dining.

## Awards & Recognition
Domaine A's flagship Cabernet Sauvignon has been a consistent feature on the Langton's Classification. The Sauvignon Blanc is widely regarded by many as Australia's finest varietal expression of the grape. The winery is often referred to in the trade as "Tasmania's First Growth". Its wines have garnered acclaim as some of Australia's finest, with some even considered to rival the best of Bordeaux.

Specific mentions include Domaine A Petit a 2017 being ranked #40 of 181 2017 Cabernet Sauvignon blends from Australia, and Domaine A Merlot 2018 ranked #31 of 74 2018 Merlot from Australia. Domaine A Stoney Vineyard Pinot Noir 2023 was ranked #69 of 86 2023 Pinot Noir from Tasmania.

## Sustainability & Certifications
No reliable information found from public sources regarding specific organic, biodynamic, or other sustainability certifications. The winery's practices include hand-plucking, hand-tying, and hand-picking of vines, and minimal intervention winemaking.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Domaine A is 105 Tea Tree Road, Campania, Tasmania, 7026. It is located approximately 34 kilometers north of Hobart, which is about a 35-minute drive. The phone number is 0484 001 944. The official website is www.domaine-a.com.au, and the cellar door email is cellardoor@domaine-a.com.au. Facilities available include a carpark, coach parking, and free Wi-Fi.

## Notable / Distinctive
Domaine A's Stoney Vineyard holds historical significance as the first commercial vineyard in Tasmania's Coal River Valley. The winery is particularly distinctive for its success with Cabernet Sauvignon and Sauvignon Blanc in Tasmania, a region where Bordeaux varieties are generally not considered to thrive. Its unique microclimate enables the production of robust, full-bodied Cabernet Sauvignon in a cool climate.

A key aspect of Domaine A's philosophy is its commitment to extended aging, often releasing wines only after many years, which contrasts with an era of instant gratification. This dedication extends to an unyielding approach where wine is not produced if the fruit quality is not perfect. The winery's reputation has led to it being referred to as "Tasmania's First Growth" by many in the wine trade.

